# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
/target/

# Generated by Tauri
# will have schema files for capabilities auto-completion
/gen/schemas

# [Why the mobile projects are not committed] Both are produced by `tauri <platform> init`
# and both build scripts run it on demand when the directory is missing
# (scripts/build/android.ps1, scripts/build/android.sh, scripts/build/ios.sh). Everything in
# them is derived from tauri.conf.json -- including `bundle.android.minSdkVersion`, which
# decides the NDK API level the toolchain compiles against. Committing the generated tree
# would put a second copy of that decision in git and let a CLI template upgrade leave a
# stale one behind.
#
# [If you need to customise the AndroidManifest or the Gradle files] Delete the rule for
# that platform, run `cargo tauri android init`, make the edit and commit `gen/android`
# from then on -- the build scripts stop calling init as soon as the directory exists.
/gen/android
# iOS can only be initialised on macOS, so this one can never be committed from a Windows
# or Linux checkout anyway.
/gen/apple

# Staged ONNX Runtime dylibs for macOS bundling (generated by build.rs)
/resources/macos/

# Third-party build artifacts (CMake/MSBuild)
/third_party/world/build_world_dll/_build/
/third_party/world/build_world_dll/_build_*/

# Third-party source code (static linking - embedded as source code in the repo)
# Note: rubberband-static/ has been removed (replaced by signalsmith-stretch)
# Generated at build time by build.rs (CoreML-compatible variant)
backend/src-tauri/resources/models/nsf_hifigan/pc_nsf_hifigan_coreml.onnx
